# .pii-allow — reviewed exemptions for the PII/secret scanner.
#
# One regex per line, matched against the WHOLE LINE. Every entry here is a
# value that was inspected and found to be synthetic. This file is deliberately
# small and specific: it exempts VALUES, never files or paths, so a real secret
# added to an exempted file is still caught.
#
# Why this file exists at all: `pii_scan.py --history` returned 26 findings, all
# of them the guardrail tests proving the guardrails detect secrets. A scan that
# can never come back clean trains everyone to ignore it — the alarm-fatigue
# failure `ai-standards/references/notification-taxonomy.md` exists to prevent.
# Exempting the fixtures is what makes the next real finding mean something.
#
# Reviewed 2026-07-26. Re-review before adding any entry: an allowlist is the
# one place where being lazy silently disables the control.
#
# NOT used here on purpose: the file-level `pii-scan: ignore-file` marker. These
# are ordinary test files that happen to contain fixtures, not dedicated corpora,
# so a whole-file exemption would blind the scanner to a real secret landing in
# them later.

# AWS's own documented example access key ID, from their public docs. It is the
# canonical "not a real key" value and cannot authenticate to anything.
# Used in: tests/guardrails_test.py, tests/unit/guardrails.test.mjs,
# tests/ci_diagnose_test.py — all asserting that output scanning catches it.
AKIAIOSFODNN7EXAMPLE

# Truncated PEM placeholders in test fixtures: a PRIVATE KEY header followed by
# an escaped \n and a body of at most a dozen base64 characters, then a closing
# quote or backslash. A real RSA key body runs to ~1600 characters, so the length
# bound is what makes this safe — a genuine key pasted into a source string is
# still flagged.
BEGIN [A-Z ]*PRIVATE KEY-----\\n(MII|MIG)[A-Za-z0-9+/=.]{0,12}["'\\]

# A PEM header as a COMPLETE quoted string — opening quote, header, closing
# quote — with no key body anywhere on the line. This is the detector's
# header→token mapping in tests/ci_diagnose_test.py, and it appears only in
# history: the current file builds the header at runtime via `_synth(...)` so the
# literal never lands in source, which is the better fix. History blobs cannot be
# edited, so they are exempted here instead.
# Safe because a header alone is not key material — the quotes bound it, so no
# base64 body can be present on the line.
["']-----BEGIN [A-Z ]+PRIVATE KEY-----["']
